course: dictionary
lesson: 6014
title: Medicate and medicated
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To medicate someone means to give them medicine, or to treat an illness with medicine. «The doctor will medicate the patient to help the infection heal.»
explain: Medicated is an adjective. It describes something that contains medicine, like «medicated cream» for a sore knee. Medicate is what someone does; medicated is what a thing is.
explain: Medicine is a safety word. Only an adult should give a child medicine, and only the right dose, which means the right amount. Never take medicine without a grown-up's say-so.
fact: Medicate is a verb: medicate, medicates, medicated, medicating.
fact: «Medicated» can be an adjective, meaning containing medicine: «medicated shampoo».
fact: «Doctors medicate patients» to treat their illnesses.
fact: To self-medicate means taking medicine on your own, without a doctor's advice. This can be dangerous.
fact: Only adults should give children medicine, and only the right dose.
exercise: «The doctor will medicate the patient to help the infection heal.» What does medicate mean here? | To paint a picture of the hospital | To take a long walk in the fresh air | *To give medicine to treat an illness
exercise: Mum buys «medicated cream» for the rash on Sam's arm. What does medicated mean? | Very cold and wet | Made of wood | *Containing medicine
exercise: Why should only an adult give a child medicine? | Because children are not allowed in the kitchen | Because medicine tastes nice | *Because it must be the right medicine and the right dose
test: «The nurse will medicate the boy.» Which word is the verb? | boy | *medicate | the | nurse
test: What does self-medicate mean? | *Taking medicine on your own, without a doctor's advice | Cooking a meal for a sick friend | Giving medicine to a friend | Taking a nap when you feel tired
exam: Which sentence uses medicated correctly? | The bus was medicated at the station. | *She put «medicated cream» on her scraped knee. | The dog medicated the ball. | Yesterday I medicated the blue sky.
